==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|targe|lang=enm}} from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|targa|lang=ang}} ''m'' and {{term|targe|lang=ang}} ''f'', both from {{etyl|non}} {{term|targa|lang=non}} "round shield" from {{proto|Germanic|targō(n)||targēn|edge|lang=en}} from {{proto|Indo-European|dArg'h-|fenced lot|lang=en}}. Akin to {{goh}} {{term|zarga|lang=goh}} "side wall, rim" ({{de}} {{term|Zarge|lang=de}} "border, frame"). Reinforced in Middle English by Old French ''[[#Old French|targe]]'' (French ''[[#French|targe]]'', Spanish ''[[tarja]]''), from the same Germanic source.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/tɑːʤ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{archaic}} A small [[shield]].
#:* '''1819''': The Jester wore his usual fantastic habit, but late accidents had led him to adopt a good cutting falchion, instead of his wooden sword, with a '''targe''' to match it — Walter Scott, ''Ivanhoe''

